{%- comment -%}
  AJ Cadence — the week as three calendar pages.

  WHAT IT IS FOR, AND WHY IT IS NOT A REPLACEMENT FOR aj-how. The explainer
  answers "what is this place". This answers a different question: "how often is
  it worth coming back". Those are both worth answering, and the second one is
  answered badly by a numbered list — "Tuesday" and "Thursday" are facts about a
  WEEK, and three anonymous cards cannot show a week. The two sections are
  deliberately shippable side by side.

  THREE CARDS, NOT SEVEN. A full seven-day strip was the first version and it
  spent five cards saying "an ordinary day" — most of the section given over to
  the days nothing special happens, and on a phone it became a scrolling deck
  where the two drops were off-screen. The rhythm this store actually has is
  three-beat: a tee on Tuesday, a bin on Thursday, and a Yoink every single day
  underneath both. So there are three pages, and the daily one sits in the
  middle because it is the one that is true on the other two days as well.

  THE EVERYDAY CARD IS THE HERO, AND IT HAS NEITHER A WEEKDAY NOR A DATE. That
  is the whole design of it: the two cards either side name a day and point at a
  numeral because you have to wait for them, and the one between them refuses to
  do either. Its bar carries the brand's loudest legal pairing — yellow on ink,
  11.17:1 — and its title carries the one accent the palette clears as type,
  because this is the loudest promise the store makes.

  THE DATES ARE REAL, AND THEN CORRECTED. Liquid renders the next Tuesday, today
  and the next Thursday from the shop's clock, which is right when the HTML is
  built and wrong the moment that HTML is cached overnight — and this is the
  most-cached page on the store. aj-cadence.js re-derives them on connect and on
  tab focus. The server render is the no-JS fallback and is complete on its own.

  TODAY COUNTS AS THE NEXT ONE. On a Tuesday the tee card reads Tuesday's date,
  not next week's, and flips its label to say it is happening now. Pointing at
  the date seven days out would be the section telling somebody to come back for
  something already on the page.

  ANCHORED AT NOON, NOT AT "NOW". Day arithmetic here is epoch seconds, and
  adding 86,400 to a timestamp near midnight lands on the wrong date on the two
  days a year the offset moves. Anchoring at midday store time puts an hour of
  daylight-saving slop nowhere near a date boundary.

  THE DAYS ARE A PROMISE THE APP KEEPS. `schedule.ts` pins TEE_DROP_WEEKDAY =
  Tuesday and BIN_DROP_WEEKDAY = Thursday at DROP_HOUR = 9 store time, because
  the weekly email says those days out loud. A theme cannot read the app's
  constants — separate repo, separate runtime — so the two days are section
  settings and the settings carry the warning. This is the class of bug this
  codebase keeps paying for: two halves each correct, nothing joining them.

  NO INK CARDS. An earlier version made the ordinary days dark and it read as a
  week that was mostly off. The pages here are paper; ink appears only as the
  border, and as the one header bar that belongs to the daily deal.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign s = section.settings

  # Absolute epoch, timezone-independent. Formatting it below renders in the
  # shop's timezone, which is what every date here should be in.
  assign now_ts = 'now' | date: '%s' | plus: 0

  # Seconds elapsed in the store's day, so midnight can be subtracted off.
  # Built in steps rather than one filter chain: Liquid applies filters strictly
  # left to right, so `h | times: 3600 | plus: m | times: 60` would multiply the
  # running total by 60 rather than the minutes.
  assign h = 'now' | date: '%H' | plus: 0
  assign m = 'now' | date: '%M' | plus: 0
  assign sec = 'now' | date: '%S' | plus: 0
  assign hours_part = h | times: 3600
  assign mins_part = m | times: 60
  assign since_midnight = hours_part | plus: mins_part | plus: sec
  assign noon_today = now_ts | minus: since_midnight | plus: 43200

  assign today_dow = 'now' | date: '%w' | plus: 0

  # The store's UTC offset in minutes, for the script. '%z' gives e.g. -0500.
  assign tz = 'now' | date: '%z'
  assign tz_sign = tz | slice: 0, 1
  assign tz_h = tz | slice: 1, 2 | plus: 0
  assign tz_m = tz | slice: 3, 2 | plus: 0
  assign tz_minutes = tz_h | times: 60 | plus: tz_m
  if tz_sign == '-'
    assign tz_minutes = 0 | minus: tz_minutes
  endif

  assign tee_dow = s.tee_day | plus: 0
  assign bin_dow = s.bin_day | plus: 0

  # Days until the next occurrence, counting today as zero.
  assign tee_ahead = tee_dow | minus: today_dow | plus: 7 | modulo: 7
  assign bin_ahead = bin_dow | minus: today_dow | plus: 7 | modulo: 7

  assign tee_offset = tee_ahead | times: 86400
  assign bin_offset = bin_ahead | times: 86400
  assign tee_ts = noon_today | plus: tee_offset
  assign bin_ts = noon_today | plus: bin_offset
-%}

<script src="{{ 'aj-cadence.js' | asset_url }}" type="module" fetchpriority="low"></script>

<div
  class="aj-cadence aj-cadence--{{ s.band }} color-{{ s.color_scheme }}"
  style="padding-block-start: {{ s.padding-block-start }}px; padding-block-end: {{ s.padding-block-end }}px;"
  {{ section.shopify_attributes }}
>
  <div class="aj-cadence__inner page-width">
    {%- if s.kicker != blank -%}
      <p class="aj-cadence__kicker">{{ s.kicker | escape }}</p>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if s.heading != blank -%}
      <h2 class="aj-cadence__heading">{{ s.heading | escape }}</h2>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if s.subheading != blank -%}
      <p class="aj-cadence__sub">{{ s.subheading | escape }}</p>
    {%- endif -%}

    <aj-cadence data-store-offset-minutes="{{ tz_minutes }}">
      {%- comment -%}
        An ordered list because the reading order is the argument: the tee, then
        the thing that happens on every day including that one, then the bin.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <ol class="aj-cadence__cards" data-cadence-scroller>
        {%- comment -%} ---- Tuesday: the tee ---- {%- endcomment -%}
        <li
          class="aj-cadence__card aj-cadence__card--tee{% if tee_ahead == 0 %} is-today{% endif %}"
          data-cadence-card
          data-cadence-weekday="{{ tee_dow }}"
        >
          <span class="aj-cadence__bar">
            {%- comment -%}
              PLURAL, because the bar names a recurring day rather than a date.
              "Tuesdays" is the promise; "Tuesday" beside a numeral reads as one
              particular Tuesday, which is the reading the numeral already
              covers. Every English weekday pluralises with a bare s, so this
              stays correct whichever day the setting names.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <span class="aj-cadence__dayname">{{ tee_ts | date: '%A' | append: 's' }}</span>
            <span class="aj-cadence__today" data-cadence-istoday {% unless tee_ahead == 0 %}hidden{% endunless %}>
              {{ s.today_label | escape }}
            </span>
          </span>
          <span class="aj-cadence__page">
            <span class="aj-cadence__date" data-cadence-date>{{ tee_ts | date: '%-d' }}</span>
            <span class="aj-cadence__title">{{ s.tee_title | escape }}</span>
            <span class="aj-cadence__body">{{ s.tee_body | escape }}</span>
            {%- if s.tee_link != blank and s.tee_cta != blank -%}
              <a class="aj-cadence__cta" href="{{ s.tee_link }}">{{ s.tee_cta | escape }}</a>
            {%- endif -%}
          </span>
        </li>

        {%- comment -%}
          ---- Every day: the Yoink ----
          NO data-cadence-weekday, deliberately. The script reads its absence as
          "this card is always today", which is the whole claim.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <li class="aj-cadence__card aj-cadence__card--daily is-today" data-cadence-card>
          <span class="aj-cadence__bar">
            <span class="aj-cadence__dayname">{{ s.daily_kicker | escape }}</span>
          </span>
          {%- comment -%}
            NO DATE NUMERAL, and its absence is the argument. A specific number
            on the card that means "every day" contradicts the card: it would
            date the Yoink to one day out of the week, which is the reading this
            whole section exists to prevent. The two weekly cards point at a
            date because you have to wait for them; this one does not, so the
            title takes the display size the numeral would have had and the card
            keeps its weight without borrowing a fact that is not true.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <span class="aj-cadence__page">
            <span class="aj-cadence__title">{{ s.daily_title | escape }}</span>
            <span class="aj-cadence__body">{{ s.daily_body | escape }}</span>
            {%- if s.daily_link != blank and s.daily_cta != blank -%}
              <a class="aj-cadence__cta" href="{{ s.daily_link }}">{{ s.daily_cta | escape }}</a>
            {%- endif -%}
          </span>
        </li>

        {%- comment -%} ---- Thursday: the bin ---- {%- endcomment -%}
        <li
          class="aj-cadence__card aj-cadence__card--bin{% if bin_ahead == 0 %} is-today{% endif %}"
          data-cadence-card
          data-cadence-weekday="{{ bin_dow }}"
        >
          <span class="aj-cadence__bar">
            <span class="aj-cadence__dayname">{{ bin_ts | date: '%A' | append: 's' }}</span>
            <span class="aj-cadence__today" data-cadence-istoday {% unless bin_ahead == 0 %}hidden{% endunless %}>
              {{ s.today_label | escape }}
            </span>
          </span>
          <span class="aj-cadence__page">
            <span class="aj-cadence__date" data-cadence-date>{{ bin_ts | date: '%-d' }}</span>
            <span class="aj-cadence__title">{{ s.bin_title | escape }}</span>
            <span class="aj-cadence__body">{{ s.bin_body | escape }}</span>
            {%- if s.bin_link != blank and s.bin_cta != blank -%}
              <a class="aj-cadence__cta" href="{{ s.bin_link }}">{{ s.bin_cta | escape }}</a>
            {%- endif -%}
          </span>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </aj-cadence>

    {%- if s.footnote != blank -%}
      <p class="aj-cadence__footnote">{{ s.footnote | escape }}</p>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
</div>

{% stylesheet %}
  /*
    THE BAND IS A SETTING, because this section's neighbours are not fixed. It
    ships next to aj-how, which owns the cyan band, and a second cyan band
    immediately under the first reads as one very tall section that lost its
    nerve halfway down. Yellow is the default: the loudest ground the brand has,
    and the only one no other homepage section is using.
  */
  .aj-cadence {
    color: var(--aj-brand-ink, #282828);

    /*
      NOTHING IN THIS SECTION MAY WIDEN THE PAGE.

      The phone fan is a horizontal scroller holding rotated, overlapping cards.
      Any one of those — the scroller's own width, a card leaning 8 degrees past
      its box, the centred card scaling up — can push past the viewport, and on
      iOS the result is not a clipped edge but a PAGE that drags sideways and
      then sits crooked with black gutters down both sides. It shipped that way
      on 6 Aug.

      `clip` rather than `hidden` on purpose: `hidden` would make this element a
      scroll container, which changes how `position: sticky` and scroll-anchoring
      behave for everything inside it. `clip` just refuses to let anything out.
    */
    overflow-x: clip;

    /*
      The pages. Warm rather than #ffffff: a pure white card on the yellow band
      looks like a hole punched in the section, and a few degrees of warmth make
      it read as paper sitting on the colour. Still 19.6:1 under ink, so nothing
      is bought with contrast.
    */
    --aj-cadence-paper: #fffdf7;
  }

  .aj-cadence--yellow {
    background: var(--aj-brand-yellow, #ffe000);
  }

  .aj-cadence--cyan {
    background: var(--aj-brand-cyan, #5cd8f8);
  }

  .aj-cadence--paper {
    background: #f6f3ea;
  }

  .aj-cadence--none {
    background: transparent;
  }

  .aj-cadence__inner {
    max-width: 68rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .aj-cadence__kicker {
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    font-family: "Ohpixel", var(--font-heading--family);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    opacity: 0.62;
  }

  .aj-cadence__heading {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: "Flashy", var(--font-heading--family);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 6.5vw, 3.25rem);
    line-height: 0.98;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .aj-cadence__sub {
    max-width: 44ch;
    margin: 0.75rem auto 0;
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.45;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  /* ---- the three pages --------------------------------------------------- */

  .aj-cadence__cards {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.25rem;
    margin: 1.75rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  /*
    THE FAN, AND THE ORDER IT IS BUILT IN. Three across only once there is room
    for three columns of real copy. Below that they stack, and the daily card
    moves to the TOP of the stack rather than staying in the middle: on a phone
    the middle of three is the one nobody scrolls to, and the daily deal is the
    only one of the three that is true today.
  */
  /*
    THREE EQUAL COLUMNS. The Yoink card was briefly given a wider column and a
    scale, and both are gone: the middle card is already distinguished three
    times over — it is the only upright one, the only one with an ink bar, and
    the only one with a coloured title — so size was a fourth signal saying the
    same thing, and it made the row lopsided rather than emphatic.
  */
  @media screen and (min-width: 900px) {
    .aj-cadence__cards {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
      gap: 1.5rem;
      align-items: stretch;
    }
  }

  /*
    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    ON A PHONE THEY ARE A FAN YOU SWIPE THROUGH
    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

    A NATIVE SCROLLER, NOT A GESTURE LIBRARY. This is a horizontal scroll
    container with snap points, which buys momentum, rubber-banding, the
    trackpad, arrow keys, screen-reader navigation and back/forward-safe scroll
    restoration — all of it correct, none of it written here. A hand-rolled
    pointer-drag deck would have to reimplement every one of those and would get
    at least one wrong.

    THE FAN IS THE CARDS OVERLAPPING, and the overlap is a negative margin on
    every card but the first, so the scroller's own padding arithmetic stays
    simple: half the scrollport minus half a card, which is what lets the first
    and last cards reach the middle.

    WHICHEVER CARD IS CENTRED COMES FORWARD AND STRAIGHTENS UP. That is a
    scroll-driven animation on the inline axis — `view(inline)` — so the card's
    own position in the scrollport drives its rotation, its scale AND its
    z-index. The z-index matters more than it looks: overlapping siblings paint
    in DOM order, so without it the right-hand card would sit on top of the
    centred one and the fan would read backwards.

    GUARDED TWICE. Browsers without scroll-driven animation keep the static
    rotations below, which is still recognisably a fan and still swipes. People
    who asked for reduced motion get the same.
  */
  @media screen and (max-width: 899px) {
    .aj-cadence__cards {
      display: flex;
      gap: 0;
      /*
        NO NEGATIVE MARGIN BLEED. This used to pull the scroller out to the
        viewport edge with `margin-inline: calc(-1 * var(--page-margin))`, which
        is only correct if that variable happens to equal the real page padding.
        Where it did not, the scroller ended up WIDER THAN THE VIEWPORT — and a
        scroll container wider than the screen does not scroll internally, it
        drags the whole document sideways. The deck still reads as continuing
        past the edge because the cards overlap and the last one is cut by the
        scrollport; it did not need the extra width to say so.
      */
      max-width: 100%;
      /*
        Block padding sized to the rotation, not guessed. A 17rem card turned
        8deg overhangs its box by about (272px / 2) x sin(8deg) ~ 19px at each
        corner, and the centred card also scales. 1.75rem clears that with a
        little to spare; anything less and a rotated corner gets shaved off by
        the scroller, which is the tell that this was done cheaply.
      */
      padding: 1.75rem 0 2rem;
      padding-inline: calc(50% - min(8.5rem, 36vw));
      overflow-x: auto;
      overscroll-behavior-x: contain;
      scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;
      scrollbar-width: none;
    }

    .aj-cadence__cards::-webkit-scrollbar {
      display: none;
    }

    .aj-cadence__card {
      flex: 0 0 min(17rem, 72vw);
      scroll-snap-align: center;
    }

    /* The overlap. On the first card it would fight the scroller's padding. */
    .aj-cadence__card + .aj-cadence__card {
      margin-inline-start: -1.75rem;
    }

    /* The no-scroll-timeline fallback, and the resting shape underneath. */
    .aj-cadence__card--tee {
      rotate: -5deg;
    }

    .aj-cadence__card--bin {
      rotate: 5deg;
    }

    .aj-cadence__card--daily {
      z-index: 2;
    }
  }

  @supports (animation-timeline: view()) {
    @media screen and (max-width: 899px) and (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
      .aj-cadence__card {
        animation: aj-cadence-fan linear both;
        animation-timeline: view(inline);
        animation-range: cover 0% cover 100%;
      }

      @keyframes aj-cadence-fan {
        0% {
          rotate: -8deg;
          scale: 0.88;
          z-index: 1;
        }

        50% {
          rotate: 0deg;
          scale: 1;
          z-index: 3;
        }

        100% {
          rotate: 8deg;
          scale: 0.88;
          z-index: 1;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  .aj-cadence__card {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    border: 3px solid var(--aj-brand-ink, #282828);
    border-radius: var(--aj-tile-radius, 8px);
    background: var(--aj-cadence-paper, #fffdf7);
    color: var(--aj-brand-ink, #282828);
    overflow: hidden;
    box-shadow: 0 8px 0 rgb(0 0 0 / 0.2);
  }

  /* The lean. Fixed per card rather than random, so it is stable across
     renders — a card that tilts differently on every reload reads as a bug. */
  @media screen and (min-width: 900px) {
    .aj-cadence__card--tee {
      rotate: -2.5deg;
    }

    .aj-cadence__card--bin {
      rotate: 2.5deg;
    }

    /* The middle page stays upright — the one the other two lean away from.
       Same size, same padding, no scale: the angle is the whole difference. */
    .aj-cadence__card--daily {
      z-index: 2;
    }
  }

  /*
    NO PAD OF PAGES BEHIND THIS CARD, and it was tried twice before being cut.
    Two extra page edges were meant to say "and another one tomorrow" without
    spending a word on it. Offset down and right, they put a paper corner beside
    the black header bar and read as a duplicate that had failed to line up;
    offset straight down, they read as a rendering fault. Both times the card
    looked broken rather than layered — which is a high price for a flourish the
    word EVERYDAY in the bar already pays for. It is one card, built exactly
    like the other two.
  */

  /*
    The title takes the display size the date numeral would have had on the
    other two, so losing the numeral does not lose the card its weight — and it
    takes the brand's purple, which is the one accent aj-brand.css clears as
    TYPE rather than as a fill. 6.80:1 on the paper, comfortably past AA.
    Yellow or cyan here would be illegible; this is the only coloured heading
    the palette actually permits.
  */
  .aj-cadence__card--daily .aj-cadence__title {
    margin-block-start: 0;
    color: var(--aj-brand-purple, #7a3fa0);
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 4.5vw, 2.625rem);
    line-height: 0.95;
  }


  /* ---- the header bar ---------------------------------------------------- */

  /*
    A REAL DESK CALENDAR'S COLOURED TOP. This is where all the colour in the
    section lives, which is what keeps three cards from becoming three
    competing blocks — the pages are identical, only the bars differ, so the
    bars ARE the legend.
  */
  .aj-cadence__bar {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;
    border-block-end: 3px solid var(--aj-brand-ink, #282828);
    /* Carried here rather than left to the card's `overflow: hidden`, because
       the daily card cannot clip — it has a stack hanging out of it. */
    border-start-start-radius: calc(var(--aj-tile-radius, 8px) - 3px);
    border-start-end-radius: calc(var(--aj-tile-radius, 8px) - 3px);
    font-family: "Ohpixel", var(--font-heading--family);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  /* Cyan carries ink, never white — aj-brand.css measures white on cyan at
     2.73:1, the mistake that looks fine on a good monitor. */
  .aj-cadence__card--tee .aj-cadence__bar {
    background: var(--aj-brand-cyan, #5cd8f8);
    color: var(--aj-brand-ink, #282828);
  }

  /* White on purple is 6.89:1. Contrast is symmetric, so this is the same
     measurement the brand file already clears for purple type on white. */
  .aj-cadence__card--bin .aj-cadence__bar {
    background: var(--aj-brand-purple, #7a3fa0);
    color: #ffffff;
  }

  /* Yellow on ink, 11.17:1 — the brand's loudest legal pairing, spent on the
     one promise that is true every single day. */
  .aj-cadence__card--daily .aj-cadence__bar {
    background: var(--aj-brand-ink, #282828);
    color: var(--aj-brand-yellow, #ffe000);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }

  .aj-cadence__dayname {
    font-weight: 400;
  }

  /* The "today" chip, on whichever of the two weekly cards has come round. */
  .aj-cadence__today {
    padding: 0.1rem 0.4rem;
    border-radius: 3px;
    background: var(--aj-brand-ink, #282828);
    color: var(--aj-brand-yellow, #ffe000);
    font-size: 0.625rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
  }

  .aj-cadence__card--daily .aj-cadence__today,
  .aj-cadence__today[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  /* ---- the page ---------------------------------------------------------- */

  .aj-cadence__page {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    display: flex;
    flex: 1;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    padding: 1.1rem 1.25rem 1.25rem;
    background: var(--aj-cadence-paper, #fffdf7);
    border-end-start-radius: calc(var(--aj-tile-radius, 8px) - 3px);
    border-end-end-radius: calc(var(--aj-tile-radius, 8px) - 3px);
  }

  .aj-cadence__date {
    font-family: "Flashy", var(--font-heading--family);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(2.75rem, 6.5vw, 3.75rem);
    line-height: 0.85;
    /* Digits in three cards side by side; they should sit on the same rhythm. */
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .aj-cadence__title {
    margin-block-start: 0.35rem;
    font-family: "Flashy", var(--font-heading--family);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    line-height: 1.05;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .aj-cadence__body {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    opacity: 0.8;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .aj-cadence__cta {
    margin-block-start: auto;
    padding-block-start: 0.75rem;
    color: inherit;
    font-family: "Ohpixel", var(--font-heading--family);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    text-underline-offset: 0.25em;
  }

  .aj-cadence__cta::after {
    content: " \2192";
  }

  .aj-cadence__footnote {
    margin: 1.15rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    opacity: 0.7;
  }

  /* ---- motion ------------------------------------------------------------ */

  /*
    TWO DIFFERENT ANSWERS, BECAUSE PHONES HAVE NO HOVER — the same split aj-how
    uses. Desktop gets a lift on hover; touch gets a scroll-driven entrance.

    THE TWO NEVER FIGHT OVER A PROPERTY. The entrance owns `translate`, the
    hover owns `scale`, and the fan owns `rotate` — CSS's independent transform
    properties, specifically so a scroll-driven animation holding its end state
    cannot be clobbered by a hover rule. Written as one `transform`, whichever
    fired last would win and the cards would jump.
  */
  .aj-cadence__card {
    transition: scale 0.18s ease, box-shadow 0.18s ease;
  }

  @media (hover: hover) and (min-width: 900px) {
    .aj-cadence__card:hover {
      scale: 1.03;
      box-shadow: 0 14px 0 rgb(0 0 0 / 0.24);
    }

  }

  .aj-cadence__card:active {
    scale: 0.99;
  }

  /*
    DESKTOP ONLY. Below 900px the cards are a fan on their own scroll timeline,
    and two animations on one element driven by two different timelines is how
    you get a card that arrives at the wrong angle. The phone gets the fan; the
    desktop gets the deal-in.
  */
  @supports (animation-timeline: view()) {
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) and (min-width: 900px) {
      .aj-cadence__card {
        animation: aj-cadence-deal linear both;
        animation-timeline: view();
        animation-range: entry 5% cover 30%;
      }

      /* Dealt onto the table, middle page last so it lands on top. */
      .aj-cadence__card--daily {
        animation-delay: -0.04s;
      }

      @keyframes aj-cadence-deal {
        from {
          opacity: 0;
          translate: 0 24px;
        }

        to {
          opacity: 1;
          translate: 0 0;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  @media screen and (max-width: 749px) {
    .aj-cadence__sub {
      font-size: 1rem;
    }
  }
{% endstylesheet %}

